<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Limits of functions with several variables- Question 1</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Assuming the limit of the function below exists, compute its value. </p>
<p style="color:blue">Note: Give your answer in exact form or correct to \(2\) decimal places.</p>
<p> \(\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y,z) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@},{@var_z@})} {@question_func@} = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p></p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>Recall, \(\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y,z) \to (a,b,c)} \frac{ f(x,y,z)}{g(x,y,z)}\)   \(\displaystyle (a,b,c) \in \mathbb{R} \) exists when \(\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y,z) \to (x,y,z)}  g(x,y,z) \neq 0\) </p>
<br>
<p>Looking at \(\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@},{@var_z@})} {@question_func@} \) the limit of the denominator exists. </p>
<p>Therefore, substituting \(x,y,z\) by \(\displaystyle {@var_x@},{@var_y@},{@var_z@}\) yields </p>
<p>\[ \displaystyle \lim_{(x,y,z) \to ({@var_x@},{@var_y@},{@var_z@})} {@question_func@} = {@ta@}\]</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2023060500</text>
